# Aerie TODO

## Locking

Lock bug — DowngradePublicLock collects all locks to release and releases them
one by one. When releasing a lock the publish function is called; however it is
possible that someone else will acquire a recursive lock as soon as a private
lock is released but before the public lock is released (and before the publish).
Fix options:
  - Release all private locks first, then public locks, OR
  - Publish all outstanding state first, then release locks.

## OSD layer

- Support multiple simultaneous filesystems on the client side.
  Currently the OSD layer does not support multiple filesystems — lock manager
  and storage-pool initialization need to happen at open/load time per storage
  system, not globally.

- DPO: implement protocol for transition between hierarchical and flat locks.
- DPO: implement read-only transactional concurrency control.

## Session / IPC

Session manager needed in the IPC layer. Session hierarchy:

    IpcSession <- DpoSession <- PxfsSession

  IpcSession: user id, effective ACL (for storage creation)
  DpoSession: pointers to journal, storage allocator

## Superblock / containers

- Superblock container: filesystem, free bitmaps.

## Namespace

- Concurrent namespace operations: mkdir, rmdir, link, unlink, rename.
- Generic namemap (parameterized by hash functor) for directory inodes and
  mount-point pseudo-inodes. Extend hashtable interface to accept hash result
  as a hint and verify correctness.
- readdir / opendir / getdents — not yet implemented in pxfs.
- Journal integration.
- Port rename to DPO.

## Inode cache optimizations

- Immutable inode optimization (e.g. for lookup):
    - If requested inode is immutable but a mutable copy exists in cache,
      do not return immutable.
    - Provide a cheap validity check for immutable inodes under contention.
    - GetInode: caller provides wrapper for immutable; mutable inodes are
      ref-counted and freed when refcount drops to 0.

- mfs/client/dir_inode — consider caching persistent entries to avoid the
  second lookup for entries already resolved. Benefit may be small because
  cache insertion adds overhead on the lookup fast path.

- client/namespace.cc:
    - Namex: use LookupFast instead of Lookup; fall back to Lookup only when
      inode is mutated.
    - nameiparent: when namex uses immutable for lookup, allow requesting a
      mutable inode directly.

- InodeMap: use a timestamp counter to signal to clients that an immutable
  inode mapping may be stale (because a mutable one was inserted).

## Storage

- Pregion should be built on top of a sparse storage pool.

## SCM model

- Integrate Mnemosyne's PCM model.
- Integrate Mnemosyne's primitives, including RAWL (log manager needs rewrite).
